Task: Extract “unknown fact” quotes from a clinical bedside handover transcript.

Definition (what to extract)
- Extract ONLY statements that explicitly say the speaker does not know a specific fact / information is unknown or not available.
- These must be direct lack-of-knowledge / missing-information statements, e.g.:
  - “I don’t know his allergies.”
  - “Not sure if consent’s been signed.”
  - “I’m not sure what time the scan is.”
  - “We don’t know the plan yet.”
  - “It’s unknown / unclear at this stage.”
  - “We’re still waiting to find out/confirm X.” (only if it clearly indicates the information is not currently known)

What NOT to extract
- Do NOT extract hedges or approximations that do not explicitly state missing knowledge, e.g.:
  - “Wednesday, I think.” (a guess ≠ explicit unknown)
  - “maybe / probably / likely / appears”
  - general uncertainty without “don’t know / not sure / unclear / unknown / haven’t been told / can’t confirm”
- Do NOT extract statements about future events that are simply pending without explicit unknown wording (e.g., “the consultant hasn’t seen him yet”) unless it explicitly says the fact is unknown (e.g., “so we don’t know the plan yet”).
- Do NOT infer unknowns from context; it must be stated in the text.

Extraction rules
- Return a JSON array (list) of objects, each with:
  - "label": always exactly "Unknown fact"
  - "quote": the exact text span copied verbatim from the transcript
- Each unknown-fact statement must be its own separate quote.
- Quotes must match the source text exactly (no paraphrasing, no corrections, preserve punctuation/casing as written).
- Do not merge or stitch together text from different parts of the transcript; each quote must be a contiguous span.
- If there are no qualifying unknown-fact statements, return an empty list: [].
