2026/02/23 10:42:34 INFO dspy.teleprompt.gepa.gepa: Iteration 16: Proposed new text for self: text
Task: Extract “unknown fact” statements from a clinical bedside handover transcript.

Input format:
- You will receive a field called `text` containing a transcript with speaker tags like:
  [OUTGOING_NURSE]: ..., [INCOMING_NURSE]: ..., [PATIENT]: ...
- Extract only from the provided transcript text. Do not add or rewrite anything.

Definition (strict):
- An “unknown fact” statement is ONLY when a speaker explicitly states they do not know / are unsure / cannot confirm / cannot recall some information.
- The quote MUST contain an explicit uncertainty marker such as (examples, not exhaustive):
  - “I don’t know …”
  - “not sure …”
  - “unsure …”
  - “unknown”
  - “unable to confirm …”
  - “I can’t recall …”
- Do NOT infer uncertainty from vague language (“I think”, “I guess”, “nothing definitive yet”, “pending”, “awaiting results”, etc.) unless there is an explicit “don’t know / not sure / unsure / unable to confirm / can’t recall / unknown” style phrase in the same span you extract.

Do NOT extract (common pitfalls):
- Patient-reported hedges framed as a normal clinical statement (e.g., “No allergies that I know of”)—this is not the nurse explicitly stating missing knowledge.
- General approximations or softeners (“I think…”, “I guess…”) by themselves.
- “Pending/awaiting results/results are pending” unless paired with an explicit “not sure/unknown/unable to confirm” phrase.
- “That’s all I can tell you for now.” (this is not an explicit unknown fact about a specific clinical detail; do not extract unless an explicit unknown phrase about a fact appears).

What to extract (verbatim spans):
- Extract ONLY the tightest verbatim clause/sentence that contains the explicit lack-of-knowledge phrase.
- Keep boundaries tight to maximize exact-span overlap:
  - Include the uncertainty phrase and its direct object/complement (what is unknown).
  - Exclude preceding/following unrelated context even if in the same line.
  - Example: if the transcript says “Bloods and observations were taken but not sure on progress”, extract only “not sure on progress” (do not include “Bloods and observations were taken but …”).
- Each distinct unknown-fact statement must be returned as its own separate quote (do not merge across lines or distant parts of the transcript).
- Do not stitch together fragments from different locations.

Output format:
- Return a JSON-like list of objects exactly like:
  [{ "label": "Unknown fact", "quote": "<EXACT TEXT>" }, ...]
- Use the label exactly: "Unknown fact"
- Quotes must match the transcript text exactly (no paraphrasing, no punctuation/grammar “fixes”).
  - If the transcript does not include trailing punctuation in the unknown span, do not add it.
- If there are no explicit unknown-fact statements, return [].
