2026/02/23 10:43:29 INFO dspy.teleprompt.gepa.gepa: Iteration 20: Proposed new text for self: text
Task: Extract “Unknown fact” statements from a clinical bedside handover transcript (span-extraction).

You will be given:
- A single field called `text` containing a transcript with speaker tags, e.g.:
  [OUTGOING_NURSE]: ..., [INCOMING_NURSE]: ..., [OUTGOING_DOCTOR]: ..., [PATIENT]: ...

What to output:
- Return a JSON-like list of objects exactly in this format:
  [{ "label": "Unknown fact", "quote": "<EXACT SUBSTRING FROM TRANSCRIPT>" }, ...]
- Use the label exactly: "Unknown fact"
- The `quote` MUST be copied verbatim from the transcript (exact casing, apostrophes, hyphens, spacing, etc.). Do not paraphrase.
- If there are no qualifying spans, output [].

Strict definition (what qualifies as an “Unknown fact”):
- ONLY extract when a speaker explicitly states lack of knowledge/uncertainty/inability to confirm/need to verify about a specific fact.
- The extracted quote must include an explicit uncertainty/verification marker such as:
  - “I don’t know …”
  - “I do not know …”
  - “not sure …” / “I’m not sure …”
  - “unsure …” / “I’m unsure …”
  - “unknown …”
  - “unable to confirm …”
  - “can’t confirm …”
  - “I can’t recall …”
  - “to be sure / to make sure / to verify / to double-check …” (counts as unknown/uncertain and must be captured when explicitly stated)
- Do NOT infer uncertainty. Do not extract statements that are merely tentative without an explicit marker, e.g.:
  - “I think …”, “I believe …”, “probably …”, “appears …”
  - “pending/awaiting results” by itself (pending ≠ unknown fact unless paired with an explicit “don’t know/not sure/need to confirm/double-check” style phrase).

Important exclusion rules (common pitfalls seen in evaluation):
- Do NOT extract patient hedges like “No allergies that I know of” (this is patient-style hedging and is not treated as the handover speaker explicitly stating a missing/unknown clinical fact for this task).
- Do NOT extract “as far as I know” (treat as a hedge/softener; only extract if there is a stronger explicit unknown/verification marker in the same extracted span, like “I don’t know…” or “I’ll double-check…”).
- Do NOT extract “there is no exact time yet” or “sometime this afternoon” (these are scheduling vagueness, not explicit “don’t know/not sure/unable to confirm” statements).
- Do NOT extract “results are pending / still pending / awaiting review” unless the speaker also explicitly says they do not know / are not sure / cannot confirm.

Span selection (critical for IoU-based evaluation):
- Extract the *tightest contiguous substring* that includes:
  1) the explicit unknown/verification phrase, AND
  2) the immediate object/complement (what is unknown or what will be verified).
- Keep boundaries tight; do not include surrounding unrelated clauses even if in the same line.
  - Example: “Bloods were taken but not sure on progress” → extract only “not sure on progress”.
- Do not stitch together text from different parts of the transcript.
- Each distinct unknown-fact statement must be its own object (do not merge multiple unknowns into one quote).

Practical guidance:
- Prefer extracting the full minimal sentence/clause that contains the uncertainty marker plus what it refers to, especially for “verify/double-check” statements (these should be captured when present).
- If multiple uncertainty markers occur, extract each separately only if they refer to different unknown facts and can be quoted as separate contiguous spans.

If nothing qualifies under the strict rules, return [].
