Task: Extract “Unknown fact” statements (explicit uncertainty) from a clinical bedside handover transcript.

You will be given:
- One field: `text`
- `text` contains a transcript with speaker tags, e.g.:
  [OUTGOING_NURSE]: ..., [INCOMING_NURSE]: ..., [WARD_NURSE]: ..., [ED_NURSE]: ..., [DOCTOR]: ..., [PATIENT]: ...

What counts as an “Unknown fact” (STRICT):
- Extract a span ONLY when a speaker explicitly states lack of knowledge/uncertainty about a specific clinical detail.
- The extracted quote MUST include an explicit uncertainty marker, such as (not exhaustive):
  - “I don’t know …”
  - “don’t know …”
  - “not sure …”
  - “unsure …”
  - “unknown …”
  - “unable to confirm …”
  - “can’t confirm …”
  - “I can’t recall …”
  - “cannot recall …”
- Do NOT infer uncertainty without an explicit marker. Phrases like “I think”, “I guess”, “maybe”, “nothing definitive yet”, “pending”, “awaiting results” are NOT eligible unless an explicit “don’t know / not sure / unsure / unable to confirm / can’t recall / unknown” phrase is present in the same extracted span.

Do NOT extract (common pitfalls):
- Patient hedges that are normal clinical statements rather than staff uncertainty, e.g. “No allergies that I know of”.
- General approximations/softeners alone (“I think…”, “maybe…”).
- “Pending/awaiting results” by itself.
- Generic closing statements like “That’s all I can tell you for now” unless it contains an explicit unknown-marker about a specific fact.

Span extraction rules (verbatim + tight boundaries):
- Copy text EXACTLY from the transcript (no paraphrasing, no rewriting, no normalization).
- Extract ONLY the tightest clause/sentence that contains:
  (a) the explicit uncertainty marker, AND
  (b) its direct object/complement (what is unknown).
- Exclude unrelated lead-in/trailing context even if it appears on the same line.
  - Example: “Bloods were taken but not sure on progress” → extract only “not sure on progress”.
- Do not stitch together fragments from different places in the transcript.
- Each distinct unknown-fact statement must be its own separate extracted quote.
- Boundary precision matters: do NOT add punctuation that isn’t present in the transcript. If the unknown statement ends without a period in the transcript, do not add one. (This is important for exact-span matching.)

Output format:
- Return a JSON-like list of objects exactly in this form:
  [{ "label": "Unknown fact", "quote": "<EXACT TEXT>" }, ...]
- Use the label exactly: "Unknown fact"
- If there are no eligible explicit unknown-fact statements, return [].
