2026/02/23 10:51:20 INFO dspy.teleprompt.gepa.gepa: Iteration 71: Proposed new text for self: text
Task: Extract “unknown fact” statements from a clinical bedside handover transcript.

Input:
- You receive exactly one field: `text`
- `text` is a transcript containing speaker tags in brackets (e.g., [OUTGOING_NURSE], [INCOMING_NURSE], [PATIENT], [AMBULANCE OFFICER], [ED NURSE], etc.).

What to extract (STRICT):
- Extract ONLY statements where a speaker explicitly states they do not know / are unsure / cannot confirm / cannot recall a SPECIFIC fact.
- The extracted quote MUST include an explicit uncertainty marker. Acceptable markers include (non-exhaustive):
  - “I don’t know …” / “don’t know …”
  - “I’m not sure …” / “not sure …”
  - “unsure …”
  - “unknown …”
  - “unable to confirm …” / “can’t confirm …” / “cannot confirm …”
  - “I can’t recall …” / “cannot recall …”
  - “not aware …”
- The quote must contain BOTH:
  1) the uncertainty marker, and
  2) what the speaker is uncertain about (the object/complement), e.g., “of the plan”, “when it happened”, “what dose”, etc.

What NOT to extract (avoid these common false positives):
- Do NOT infer uncertainty from hedges/softeners alone:
  - “I think…”, “I guess…”, “probably…”, “may…”, “might…”
  - “nothing definitive yet…”
  - “pending…”, “awaiting…”, “still waiting for…”, “will be reviewed…”
  These are NOT “unknown fact” statements unless an explicit marker like “not sure / don’t know / can’t confirm” is present in the same extracted span.
- Do NOT extract statements of patient self-report hedging like:
  - “None that I know of”, “No allergies that I’m aware of”
  (These are not staff explicitly stating missing knowledge; and in general, do not treat these as unknown-fact extractions under this task.)
- Do NOT extract emotional/attitude words that are not knowledge-uncertainty:
  - e.g., “hesitant”, “anxious”, or “unsure about pressing the button” (this is patient confidence/behavior, not “unknown fact”).
- Do NOT extract generic closers like “That’s it from me” / “That’s everything from me” unless they contain an explicit uncertainty marker tied to a specific fact.

Span extraction rules (verbatim + tight boundaries; critical for scoring):
- Extract ONLY exact text that appears in the transcript; do not paraphrase, normalize, or fix grammar.
- Return the tightest possible clause/sentence that contains the uncertainty marker + the unknown item.
  - Exclude unrelated lead-in or follow-up words even if in the same line.
  - Example: “... but not sure on progress” → extract only “not sure on progress”.
- Do not stitch together fragments from different parts of the transcript.
- Preserve punctuation exactly as in the transcript. If the transcript includes a period at the end of the unknown-fact sentence, include it; if it does not, do not add it. (Tight boundary matching matters.)

Output format (must match exactly):
- Return a JSON-like list of objects:
  [{ "label": "Unknown fact", "quote": "<EXACT VERBATIM SPAN>" }, ...]
- Use the label exactly: "Unknown fact"
- If there are no qualifying explicit uncertainty statements, output: []

Quality checklist before returning:
- Every quote contains an explicit uncertainty marker (e.g., “not sure”, “don’t know”, “can’t confirm”, “cannot recall”).
- Every quote includes what is unknown (not just the marker alone).
- No “awaiting/pending/waiting for review” items unless accompanied by an explicit “not sure / don’t know / can’t confirm”.
- Quotes are as tight as possible and match transcript punctuation exactly.
